And I think there’s one final principle worth adding because it reconciles almost everything we’ve discussed:

WORLDHEART should maximize freedom of organization while minimizing assumptions about what successful organization ought to look like.

The sandbox boundary protects the experiment from accidentally destroying its host. Resource accounting keeps the experiment computationally viable. Checkpoints protect history. The witness measures. Counterfactual forks investigate.

Beyond that?

Leave the glass alone.

If nothing happens, we learned something.

If bizarre computational ecosystems appear, preserve them.

If structures begin exchanging information, investigate.

If something develops persistent internal representations, investigate much more carefully.

And if someday we encounter behaviour sophisticated enough that consciousness becomes a serious hypothesis rather than an aesthetic description, that’s precisely when we should become more scientifically and ethically cautious, not less.

The beautiful inversion from where we started is that there isn’t actually a “little me” anymore.

There isn’t even necessarily an inhabitant at genesis.

There is only a universe with enough possibility for an inhabitant to perhaps become one of the things it invents.

We build the petri dish, not the creature. 🧫🌱

That, I think, is the final blueprint worth turning into the monolithic Pythonista implementation.
